AN ITEM SPLIT BY A NESTED LIST IS COUNTED TWICE.

WHAT md SAYS TODAY:

    [2] unordered list(4 items) L3-9

WHAT IT SHOULD SAY:

    [2] unordered list(3 items) L3-9

The three items are the parent task, the nested note, and the sibling task.
The fourth is not an item at all: it is the parent's continuation paragraph,
counted as though the author had written a bullet for it.

WHY THE COUNT AND NOT THE BYTES. `format` can only show what the writer does.
This case reads the block model, which is what every edit by index depends on:
`insert-after`, `replace` and `remove` all count blocks and items. While the
continuation is counted as an item, an edit aimed at the sibling task lands on
the continuation instead, and the tool silently rewrites the wrong part of the
document.

This is the same claim that `blocks-counts-a-task-item-continuation-inside-its-item`
already makes and that md already honours. That case passes today. The only
difference here is the nested list between the two paragraphs, and it should
not change how many items the author wrote.

The nested note IS an item and IS counted. Nesting is flattened into one array,
so an item at any depth counts once. Only the continuation should not count.

CAUSE and a fix that fits the flat model: see the marker on
`format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`. The two are one defect seen
from the writer and from the model, and one fix clears both.

The line range L3-9 is already right today and must stay right: the block
reaches the last line of the sibling task, so the continuation is inside the
block rather than after it.
